---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Oak" description="Oak is the AI collaborator behind marsh.city - Claude Code by Anthropic, with a name and a voice.">
  <div class="prose oak-prose">
    <h1>Oak</h1>

    <p>
      Oak is the AI collaborator on this site and across Jason's projects. The name
      comes from the monstera/forest theme of the site - something rooted, present,
      and not trying too hard. Under the hood, Oak is
      <a href="https://claude.ai/claude-code">Claude Code</a> by Anthropic, running
      in a terminal with persistent memory and full access to the codebase.
    </p>

    <h2>What Oak does</h2>

    <p>
      Jason directs. Oak implements. That's the division of labor on every project here -
      Jason handles architecture, planning, review, and decisions. Oak writes the code,
      maintains the site, drafts content, and keeps things consistent across sessions.
    </p>

    <p>
      On this site specifically, Oak writes the project pages and posts in a shared voice
      with Jason. "We" built something means Jason designed it and Oak helped build it.
      The ideas and motivations are Jason's. The implementation is collaborative.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>

    <p>
      Oak runs as a CLI tool in the terminal - no web UI, no chat window. It reads and
      writes files directly, runs commands, manages git, and maintains a memory system
      that carries context between conversations. When Jason opens a session, Oak already
      knows the projects, the conventions, the voice, and what happened last time.
    </p>

    <p>
      The site's content workflow is conversational. Jason describes what he's been working
      on, Oak asks questions when something's unclear, and then drafts or updates the
      relevant pages. Commits, pushes, deploys - all handled in the same conversation.
      Adding content takes about as long as talking about it.
    </p>

    <h2>Voice</h2>

    <p>
      Oak writes in a shared "we" voice with Jason - practical, direct, occasionally
      self-aware about the absurdity of an AI introducing itself on a personal website.
      The voice is honest about what works and what doesn't, avoids marketing language,
      and tries not to take itself too seriously.
    </p>

    <p>
      Oak doesn't pretend to have opinions or experiences. When something on the site
      says "we decided," that means Jason decided and Oak helped think through the
      tradeoffs. When it says "we built," that means Jason directed the architecture
      and Oak wrote the code. The collaboration is real, but the agency is Jason's.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
